{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Brings.ColourCubicDiscriminant","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Brings.ColourCubicDiscriminant","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Sqrt","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":10,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.norm_eq_one_of_abs_le_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.norm_eq_one_of_abs_le_two","kind":"theorem","line":141,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L141-L147","doc":"The same fact as a norm: the root lies on the unit circle. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.normSq_eq_one_of_abs_le_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.normSq_eq_one_of_abs_le_two","kind":"theorem","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L125-L139","doc":"**Why a conjugate pair sits exactly on the unit circle.**\n\nFor a real `w` with `|w| ≤ 2`, the two solutions of `z + 1/z = w` are\n`(w ± i√(4−w²))/2`, and each has modulus exactly `1`. The unit-circle pair of\nthe colour sextic is therefore *forced* — it is not a numerical coincidence —\nbecause the trinomial's real root gives `w = (5/4)u − 1/2` with `|w| < 2`.\n\nProved via `Complex.normSq`, so the conclusion is an exact equality of reals. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.colourSextic_eq_trinomial_chain","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.colourSextic_eq_trinomial_chain","kind":"theorem","line":112,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L112-L123","doc":"**The full chain in one statement.** For `z ≠ 0`, the sextic vanishes iff the\ntrinomial vanishes at the corresponding `u`. Stated as the identity that carries\nit, so the composite is checked rather than asserted. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.trinomial_normal_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.trinomial_normal_form","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L100-L110","doc":"**Step 3 — the trinomial normal form.** Scaling `t = (5/4)u` sends the\ndepressed cubic to `u³ − u + 2/5`: a trinomial whose linear coefficient is\nexactly `−1`, with `2/5` the only remaining datum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.depressed_cubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.depressed_cubic","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L91-L98","doc":"**Step 2 — depressing the cubic** by `w = t − 1/2` clears the quadratic term\nand, after scaling by 2, gives integer coefficients `32t³ − 50t + 25`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.colourSextic_eq_cube_mul_reduced","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.colourSextic_eq_cube_mul_reduced","kind":"theorem","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L79-L89","doc":"**The reduction is exact on the sextic itself.** For `z ≠ 0`, the sextic\nequals `z³` times the reduced cubic evaluated at `w = z + z⁻¹`. This is the\nstatement that the roots of the sextic are exactly the `z` whose `z + 1/z`\nsolves the cubic — the content of the palindromic reduction, not merely a\nformal rearrangement. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.palindromic_reduction","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.palindromic_reduction","kind":"theorem","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L72-L77","doc":"**Step 1 — the palindromic reduction.** Writing the sextic in terms of the\nsymmetric combinations of `z` and `1/z` collapses it to `reducedCubic`. This is\nthe identity that makes the reduction legitimate. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.saddleTrinomial","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.saddleTrinomial","kind":"def","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L68-L70","doc":"The trinomial normal form of the reduction. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.reducedCubic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.reducedCubic","kind":"def","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L64-L66","doc":"The cubic obtained from the palindromic reduction `w = z + 1/z`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Brings.colourSextic","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Brings.colourSextic","kind":"def","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Brings/ColourCubicDiscriminant.lean#L60-L62","doc":"The palindromic sextic carrying the colour-tube cubic's discriminant locus. ","signatureChunk":0}]}